WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.670
Our weather club measured

2
00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:03.170
rain for a whole month.

3
00:00:03.890 --> 00:00:06.470
Then I asked: which week was the wettest?

4
00:00:07.110 --> 00:00:11.550
The sheet said “a lot”, “12”, “12 mm”

5
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:13.590
and “heavy”, and the dates were

6
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:15.070
written three different ways!

7
00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:19.010
Today: start with a question, then

8
00:00:19.010 --> 00:00:20.850
collect data that can answer it.

9
00:00:21.930 --> 00:00:24.290
Good data starts with a question.

10
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:26.640
Which week had the most rain?

11
00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.090
How long do pupils walk to school?

12
00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.160
The question tells you what to collect,

13
00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:35.120
and just as important, what not to

14
00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:35.440
collect.

15
00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:38.580
Then plan the sheet before

16
00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.860
anyone writes a thing.

17
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:43.270
One column per fact, with the unit in the

18
00:00:43.270 --> 00:00:45.870
heading, like rain in millimetres.

19
00:00:46.490 --> 00:00:50.330
Fixed codes, like P, A and L for

20
00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:52.350
present, absent and late.

21
00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.560
And every date written the same way.

22
00:00:56.740 --> 00:00:58.880
On paper, tally marks are the

23
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:00.800
quickest way to count: four

24
00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:03.120
strokes, then a fifth across them.

25
00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:05.690
Later, type the counts into a

26
00:01:05.690 --> 00:01:07.570
spreadsheet, one row at a time.

27
00:01:08.710 --> 00:01:10.950
Most spreadsheets can save a file as

28
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.250
CSV, comma-separated values.

29
00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.510
It's plain text: one line per row, with

30
00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:19.730
commas between the values, and the

31
00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.090
column names on the first line.

32
00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:23.120
Any program can open it,

33
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:25.000
today or in twenty years.

34
00:01:26.300 --> 00:01:28.000
One tip from the computer room.

35
00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.060
When your sheet has Arabic

36
00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:33.920
names, save it as CSV UTF-8.

37
00:01:34.630 --> 00:01:36.590
Otherwise the letters can arrive as

38
00:01:36.590 --> 00:01:38.590
nonsense on someone else's computer.

39
00:01:39.960 --> 00:01:40.360
Quick check!

40
00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:43.020
Our club wants to know which week

41
00:01:43.020 --> 00:01:44.420
this term had the most rain.

42
00:01:45.060 --> 00:01:47.440
Which data does that question really need?

43
00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:50.950
The date and the rain in millimetres:

44
00:01:51.530 --> 00:01:52.550
that's all the question needs.

45
00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:55.640
Names and photos don't help it,

46
00:01:55.820 --> 00:01:57.000
so don't collect them.

47
00:01:57.560 --> 00:01:59.580
Less data is quicker to collect,

48
00:02:00.080 --> 00:02:02.340
easier to check, and safer to keep.

49
00:02:03.640 --> 00:02:05.640
How you save data also decides

50
00:02:05.640 --> 00:02:07.080
what it costs to send.

51
00:02:07.770 --> 00:02:10.910
The same attendance as JSON repeats every

52
00:02:10.910 --> 00:02:14.170
column name in every record, so it's more

53
00:02:14.170 --> 00:02:15.890
than twice the size of CSV.

54
00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.930
On a slow 2G connection, a school's term

55
00:02:19.930 --> 00:02:21.870
of it takes nearly five minutes;

56
00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:24.290
compressed, about thirteen seconds.

57
00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.580
In the app, choose how much data,

58
00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:29.780
the format and the network.

59
00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:32.560
See the file's size and how

60
00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:33.700
long it takes to download.

61
00:02:34.430 --> 00:02:36.250
How would you send your school's term?

62
00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:37.960
Your turn.

63
00:02:38.570 --> 00:02:42.190
Our club wrote “twelve”, “twelve mm” and

64
00:02:42.190 --> 00:02:44.590
“a lot” for the same kind of day.

65
00:02:45.130 --> 00:02:46.150
What would have stopped that?

66
00:02:47.390 --> 00:02:49.270
A planned sheet, with the unit

67
00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:51.030
in the heading and numbers only,

68
00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:52.090
would have stopped it.

69
00:02:52.570 --> 00:02:54.610
Remember: start from a question.

70
00:02:55.370 --> 00:02:57.430
Plan the sheet before you collect.

71
00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:00.020
Collect only what you need.

72
00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:02.370
And save it in a format others

73
00:03:02.370 --> 00:03:04.570
can open and afford to send.

74
00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:07.960
Try it in class tomorrow: ask your

75
00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.920
pupils one question, like how long it

76
00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:12.080
takes them to get to school.

77
00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.600
Agree on the columns, the units

78
00:03:14.600 --> 00:03:15.720
and the codes together.

79
00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:17.920
Collect the answers with tally

80
00:03:17.920 --> 00:03:19.700
marks, then type them into a

81
00:03:19.700 --> 00:03:21.460
sheet and save it as CSV.
